A-12 ambient-light torch control, A/B on the zxing_api36 emulator (Android 16),
with the regression checks on DN-ENG41 and both emulators. 2026-11-02

Measured with tools/maint/flap-light-sensor.sh: the front light set to AUTO, then
the emulator's light sensor driven across both thresholds -- 5 lux and 900 lux,
alternating every half second for six seconds. A physical device's light sensor
cannot be driven from here, which is why this one is on an emulator.

What is counted is CameraConfiguration's own log line, one per actual change of the
camera's flash mode. That is the thing with a cost: CameraManager.setTorch tears
down the AutoFocusManager and builds a new one around every one of them.

  BEFORE: 6 flash-mode changes in 6 seconds
    [torch, on]  x6
  AFTER:  1
    [torch, on]

The after-run was repeated three times, twice with the scratch script and once with
the committed one, and gave 1 every time; the before-run was reproduced with the
committed script too.

Note what the emulator cannot show. Its camera reports no supported flash modes, so
the torch never actually comes on and getTorchState keeps returning false. That is
why only the "on" direction appears in the log: a setTorch(false) matches the state
the camera reports and is dropped before it logs anything. It makes the before-count
a count of on-attempts rather than of toggles, which is if anything the kinder
reading of the old code.

== The rule itself ==

TorchPolicy carries the decision, away from the camera and the sensor, and has 8
unit tests in :scanner. They were checked for teeth: deleting the interval check
fails 4 of them.

  $ ./gradlew :scanner:testDebugUnitTest
  77 tests, 0 failures            (69 before, + 8 TorchPolicyTest)

  with the interval check deleted: 77 tests, 4 failures --
    aFlipBackIsHeldOffUntilTheIntervalHasPassed
    theIntervalIsBetweenChanges_notSinceTheFirstOne
    resetMakesTheNextDecisionImmediateAgain
    aShorterIntervalIsHonoured

